<p>
A year ago, I learned from my friend Watts Martin <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=12921">that according to LinkedIn <del>know nothings</del> influencers, the use of em-dashes is a sure sign of AI generated text</a>. Real people, they said, use hyphens so whenever you see an em-dash, you can be sure that the text was written by an AI. Martin and I had a good laugh and largely moved on.
</p>
<p>
A couple of weeks ago, Martin published a post <a href="https://coyotetracks.org/blog/leveraging-ai-in-writing/">about the use of AI in writing</a>. Martin is a tech writer and a published SF author so this is an area he knows and cares about. One of the points he emphasizes is that <i>by definition</i> text generated by AI represents average writing.
</p>
<p>
If you do any sort of professional writing, you should give Martin&rsquo;s post a look. If you care about good writing, you should give Martin&rsquo;s post a look. Even if you don&rsquo;t fall into either of those cohorts, there&rsquo;s a lesson for you in the post.
</p>
<p>
As I was reading his post it popped into my mind that if AI produces definitionally average text and if AI generated text uses em-dashes, then those who <i>don&rsquo;t</i> use em-dashes must be below average writers.
</p>
<p>
Okay, it&rsquo;s not quite a syllogism and the conclusion is, in any event, self evident but it&rsquo;s another indication of how silly this movement to avoid using em-dashes—lest you be accused of having AI doing your writing—is. Good writers aren&rsquo;t afraid to use em-dashes. Probably some good writers don&rsquo;t use them at all—Irreal, of course, doesn&rsquo;t understand how this could be—but lots do and it&rsquo;s probably safe to say that those who do are showing that they care about their writing..
</p>
<p>
So, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13513">again</a>, use em-dashes as you see fit and tell the naysayers to go pound sand.</p>
